Contract: runtime.c documents pm_runtime_barrier returning 1 only if a resume
request is still pending and it must wake the device; otherwise it returns 0.
pm_runtime_work clears request_pending and resumes the device asynchronously.
runtime-test.c:177 assumes the worker cannot finish between pm_runtime_get
and pm_runtime_barrier, but does not exclude that scheduling order.

RED: the unchanged EvoCC expanded-KUnit Image still fails exactly that original
assertion; the paired GCC Image passes it in this run. Both boot and shut down.
That timing difference is retained, not converted to a test PASS.

GREEN reduction: a real signed GPL kernel module creates a no-callback device.
Across 64 get/barrier/put cycles, flush_work deterministically selects the
completed-worker order on 32 cycles. Those 32 barriers must return 0; all
remaining barriers must return either 0 or 1. Every cycle separately checks
the active state and subsequent successful synchronous suspension.
Module loading fails on a violation; the loader checks actual load/unload.

